{
 "id": "l29",
 "label": "29",
 "title": "The word when in Tagalog",
 "summary": "Kailán in questions, nang / noóng for the past, kung / kapág (pag) for the present and future, and kung + question words",
 "minutes": 30,
 "goals": [
  "Use kailán to ask when",
  "Use nang or noóng for a past action",
  "Use kung or kapág (pag) for a present or future action, like English if",
  "Pair kung with question words: kung síno, kung anó, kung saán…"
 ],
 "sections": [
  {
   "title": "Many ways to say when",
   "blocks": [
    {
     "type": "p",
     "t": "English has one word, *when*. Tagalog translates it in **several ways**, depending on what you mean:"
    },
    {
     "type": "table",
     "head": [
      "Tagalog",
      "Used for",
      "Example"
     ],
     "rows": [
      [
       "[[kailán|other]]",
       "a **question**",
       "Kailán ka áalis? *When will you leave?*"
      ],
      [
       "[[nang|other]] or [[noón|other]]",
       "an action in the **past**",
       "Nang ako'y maliít… *When I was small…*"
      ],
      [
       "[[kung|other]] or [[kapág|other]]",
       "an action in the **present or future**, similar to English *if*",
       "Kung áalis ka… *When / If you leave…*"
      ]
     ],
     "title": "Which when?"
    },
    {
     "type": "box",
     "kind": "english",
     "title": "Think about the time",
     "t": "Ask yourself: is it a **question**? Use kailán. Did it already happen? Use nang or noóng. Is it happening now, or still to come? Use kung or kapág."
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "title": "I. Kailán",
   "blocks": [
    {
     "type": "p",
     "t": "When *when* starts a **question**, it is translated with **kailán** (see Lesson 9)."
    },
    {
     "type": "examples",
     "items": [
      {
       "tl": "Kailán ka áalis?",
       "en": "When will you leave?",
       "parts": [
        [
         "Kailán",
         "when",
         "other"
        ],
        [
         "ka",
         "you",
         "pron"
        ],
        [
         "áalis",
         "will leave",
         "pred"
        ]
       ]
      },
      {
       "tl": "Kailán ka dumatíng?",
       "en": "When did you arrive?",
       "parts": [
        [
         "Kailán",
         "when",
         "other"
        ],
        [
         "ka",
         "you",
         "pron"
        ],
        [
         "dumatíng",
         "arrived",
         "pred"
        ]
       ]
      }
     ],
     "title": "Kailán questions"
    },
    {
     "type": "box",
     "kind": "tip",
     "title": "Any tense",
     "t": "Kailán works with a future verb (áalis, *will leave*) and with a past verb (dumatíng, *arrived*)."
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "title": "II. Nang or noóng",
   "blocks": [
    {
     "type": "p",
     "t": "To show an action that happened in the **indefinite or vague past**, *when* is translated with either **nang** or **noóng** (**noón** + ligature **-g**). Both mean the same here, so the book gives each sentence with either word."
    },
    {
